Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.90,0:00:03.18,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Put living cells under a microscope,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:03.22,0:00:06.88,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}and a camera sees almost nothing: they're nearly transparent.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:06.95,0:00:09.08,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Yet the light that went through them changed a lot.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:09.12,0:00:12.78,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Not in brightness, but in phase: the cells delayed it.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:12.85,0:00:15.80,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}So how do you photograph something a camera can't see?
Dialogue: 0,0:00:15.87,0:00:17.01,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}That's the phase problem.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:17.05,0:00:20.57,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Its solutions are holography and phase retrieval.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:21.39,0:00:25.62,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Remember: at each point, light is an arrow, a complex number.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:25.66,0:00:28.77,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Its length is the amplitude, its angle the phase.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:28.84,0:00:31.92,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}A cell slows light down slightly. Where it's thicker,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:31.96,0:00:35.39,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}the light falls further behind, so its arrow turns further.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:35.46,0:00:38.66,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}But a camera can't record arrows. It records brightness,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:38.70,0:00:41.83,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}the squared length |U|², and throws every angle away.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:41.90,0:00:44.42,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Why not measure the angle? Every arrow spins:
Dialogue: 0,0:00:44.46,0:00:47.95,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}green light oscillates 600 trillion times a second,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:47.99,0:00:50.42,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}far too fast for any detector.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:50.49,0:00:54.16,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}So for a transparent object, all the information sits in the phase,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:54.20,0:00:56.59,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}the very part the camera loses.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:58.21,0:01:03.06,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Dennis Gabor's idea, in 1948: add a known reference wave, R.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:03.10,0:01:07.41,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}It spins in step with U, so the angle between them holds still.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:07.48,0:01:10.02,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}The camera sees the length of the sum, U + R.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:10.06,0:01:13.65,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Change U's phase, and that length grows and shrinks.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:13.72,0:01:15.97,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}The phase is now written in brightness.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:16.84,0:01:21.02,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Expanding the square gives four terms. The two cross terms,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:21.06,0:01:24.74,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}UR* and U*R, contain U itself, phase and all.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:24.81,0:01:27.81,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}With the reference straight on, as in Gabor's setup,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:27.85,0:01:30.20,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}all four terms pile up in one place,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:30.24,0:01:32.75,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}and the twin, U*, spoils the picture.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:32.82,0:01:36.40,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}In 1962, Leith and Upatnieks tilted the reference.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:36.44,0:01:40.19,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Now the camera records fine stripes, called fringes.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:40.26,0:01:44.63,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}A tilt is a shift in spatial frequency. So in the spectrum,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:44.67,0:01:48.00,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}UR* slides to one side, and its twin to the other.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:48.82,0:01:51.56,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Here's a computed off-axis hologram of our cells.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:51.60,0:01:55.33,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Take its Fourier transform, and three islands appear.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:55.40,0:01:57.55,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}The middle island holds |U|² and |R|².
Dialogue: 0,0:01:57.59,0:02:02.58,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}On either side sits one cross term: UR*, and its twin.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:02.65,0:02:05.92,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}They mustn't overlap. The middle one is twice as wide,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:05.96,0:02:08.11,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}so the tilt's frequency must be at least
Dialogue: 0,0:02:08.15,0:02:10.19,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}three times the object's bandwidth.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:10.26,0:02:14.18,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Now crop one island, move it to the centre, and transform back.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:14.85,0:02:16.56,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Out comes the full complex field:
Dialogue: 0,0:02:16.60,0:02:20.05,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}brightness shows amplitude, and colour shows phase.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:20.12,0:02:22.56,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}We recorded this hologram out of focus.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:22.60,0:02:24.65,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}But with the whole field in hand,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:24.69,0:02:28.80,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}the angular spectrum method from episode one can propagate it anywhere.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:28.87,0:02:31.69,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Sweep the distance, and the cells snap into focus,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:31.73,0:02:34.11,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}long after the picture was taken.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:35.73,0:02:37.61,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}What if there's no reference, only brightness?
Dialogue: 0,0:02:37.65,0:02:40.28,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Then you lean on what you already know.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:40.35,0:02:43.05,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Here, light passes through a window of known shape,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:43.09,0:02:44.95,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}and the sample is transparent.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:44.99,0:02:48.23,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}So in this plane, every arrow's length is known.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:48.30,0:02:51.91,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}A lens shows the far field, and a camera there gives its lengths too.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:51.95,0:02:54.99,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Lengths in both planes, angles in neither.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:55.06,0:02:57.73,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}The Gerchberg–Saxton algorithm starts from random
Dialogue: 0,0:02:57.77,0:03:01.72,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}angles and goes back and forth with Fourier transforms.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:01.79,0:03:05.69,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}In each plane, it keeps the angles, but swaps in the lengths it knows.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:05.76,0:03:07.99,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Each swap puts the guess on one constraint.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:08.03,0:03:12.88,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Bouncing between them, it homes in on a field that satisfies both.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:12.95,0:03:16.40,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Watch the error fall, as the cells emerge from the noise.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:17.27,0:03:22.34,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}The far field alone has blind spots: a shifted copy, a flipped twin,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:22.38,0:03:25.58,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}or a constant phase gives exactly the same brightness.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:25.65,0:03:28.13,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Our lopsided window rules out the first two.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:28.17,0:03:30.14,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}More diverse data helps too:
Dialogue: 0,0:03:30.18,0:03:34.60,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}several distances, or the overlapping scans of ptychography.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:35.42,0:03:37.66,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Now run it backwards. Instead of measuring
Dialogue: 0,0:03:37.70,0:03:40.47,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}a far field, ask for one: say, a λ.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:40.54,0:03:42.18,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}The mask can only change phase,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:42.22,0:03:45.58,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}like a liquid-crystal screen or an etched glass plate.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:45.62,0:03:48.96,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Same loop, with the target in place of the measurement.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:49.03,0:03:53.25,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Watch the mask's phase scramble, and the far field snap into the target.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:53.92,0:03:57.16,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}The grain is speckle. The target fixes only brightness,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:57.20,0:03:59.78,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}so the far-field phase comes out random,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:59.82,0:04:03.80,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}and nearby points interfere with each other, as in episode four.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:03.87,0:04:06.73,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Modern designs often use gradient descent instead,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:06.77,0:04:09.06,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}which we'll meet in episode ten.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:09.88,0:04:12.96,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}On a computer, each idea is just a few lines.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:13.03,0:04:16.39,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Holography: FFT the hologram, crop the island,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:16.43,0:04:20.94,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}inverse FFT, then refocus with the angular spectrum method.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:21.01,0:04:25.53,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Retrieval and design: a loop of FFTs that swaps in what you know.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:25.60,0:04:28.76,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Two pitfalls. Every fringe needs at least two pixels,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:28.80,0:04:32.97,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}or the island runs off the edge of the spectrum and wraps around.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:33.04,0:04:37.41,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}So off-axis holography spends part of the camera's pixels on fringes.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:37.48,0:04:38.98,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}And Gerchberg–Saxton can stall.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:39.02,0:04:43.15,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Restart it from new random angles, or switch to gradients.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:43.97,0:04:47.37,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Cameras lose phase. Interference writes it into fringes,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:47.41,0:04:51.11,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}and constraints let a computer recover it, or design it.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:51.18,0:04:53.50,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}Where does it break? Holography needs steady,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:53.54,0:04:56.95,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}coherent light, and retrieval needs diverse data.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:57.02,0:04:58.99,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}We just designed a phase mask on a computer.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:59.03,0:05:02.53,Default,,0,0,0,,{\fad(120,120)}How do you build one, and what does "thin" really mean?
